{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"San Francisco, CA","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"San Francisco Rent Ordinance just cause eviction requirements","requirement":"A landlord of a unit covered by the Rent Ordinance must have a 'just cause' reason that is the dominant motive for the eviction. Expiration of a rental agreement or a change in ownership is not just cause. Section 37.9(a) lists 17 just causes: non-payment or habitual late payment of rent; uncured substantial breach; nuisance; illegal use of the unit; refusal to sign a renewal on materially the same terms; refusal of landlord access; unapproved subtenant remaining at the end of the term; owner or family occupancy; condominium conversion sale; demolition or permanent removal of a unit; capital improvements or rehabilitation; substantial rehabilitation of a building at least 50 years old; Ellis Act withdrawal; lead remediation; development agreement under Admin. Code Ch. 56; expired Good Samaritan tenancy; and a development project requiring demolition under Planning Code Section 317. A just cause reason is also needed to remove housing services such as garage, parking, storage and laundry. Landlords that comply with Section 37.2(r) may temporarily remove services for mandatory soft-story seismic retrofit work. Unlawful evictions or attempts may expose the landlord to civil and/or criminal liability.","key_value":"17 just cause reasons under Section 37.9(a)","coverage_conditions":"Rental units covered by the Rent Ordinance. Some tenancies exempt from the rent increase limits (units whose first Certificate of Occupancy was issued after June 13, 1979, tenancies eligible for a rent increase under Costa-Hawkins, and some tenancies whose rent is regulated by another government agency) remain subject to the just cause eviction provisions.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":"Substantial civil and/or criminal liability for unlawful eviction or attempted eviction.","citation":"S.F. Admin. Code § 37.9(a)","quoted_span":"In order to evict a tenant from a rental unit covered by the Rent Ordinance, a landlord must have a \"just cause\" reason that is the dominant motive for pursuing the eviction.","supporting_spans":["Note that the mere expiration of a rental agreement or a change in ownership does not constitute \"just cause\" for eviction."],"confidence":0.9,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"San Francisco, CA","level":"city","category":"rent_increase_limits","status":"in_force","title":"San Francisco Rent Ordinance rent increase limitations: coverage and exemptions","requirement":"The Rent Ordinance limits rent increases for covered units. The page gives no formula or amount. It states that some tenancies are exempt from the rent increase limitations: units that first obtained a Certificate of Occupancy after June 13, 1979, tenancies eligible for a rent increase under the Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act, and some tenancies where the rent is regulated by another government agency. These tenancies remain subject to the Ordinance's just cause eviction provisions.","key_value":"Units first obtaining a Certificate of Occupancy after June 13, 1979 are exempt from the rent increase limits","coverage_conditions":"Rental units covered by the Rent Ordinance, other than those exempt from the rent increase limitations.","exemptions":"Units that first obtained a Certificate of Occupancy after June 13, 1979; tenancies eligible for a rent increase under the Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act; some tenancies where the rent is regulated by another government agency.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":{"field":"certificate_of_occupancy","op":">","value":"1979-06-13"}},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act (state law) makes some tenancies eligible for rent increases despite the Ordinance.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"S.F. Admin. Code ch. 37 (Rent Ordinance)","quoted_span":"This includes tenancies in newly constructed rental units that first obtained a Certificate of Occupancy after June 13, 1979","supporting_spans":["Some tenancies that are exempt from the rent increase limitations of the Ordinance are still subject to the eviction provisions of the Ordinance"],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"Source is an official SF.gov overview page for just cause evictions. It gives no rent increase formula, effective dates or relocation assistance, so those fields are null. The rent_increase_limits record rests on the coverage and exemption statements only, and the citation is the chapter-level Rent Ordinance (Admin. Code ch. 37) because the page cites no specific section for the rent increase limits. Costa-Hawkins and other-agency exemptions are kept in the exemptions text only, not in coverage_logic."}
